;;; A pair contradiction's core names only its positive side, where that side
;;; is a fact the program states — M1e2 **W21**, register row
;;; `W21/pair-core-root-given` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1e2.2.21 (2026-11-05) from S1e2.2.19 § Notes.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One candidate, `(q a)`, which the hrule proposes. Committed, it derives
;;; `(not (p a))` by `neg`, and the program states `(p a)`: a pair
;;; contradiction whose positive side is root's and whose negative side the
;;; commitment derives.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is {∅}: root is alive, and {(q a)} is dead, N's one member. The pair
;;; rests on both its sides: `(p a)`, stated, and `(not (p a))`, which `neg`
;;; derives from `(q a)`. So the core is `(p a) (q a)`, which names the
;;; commitment, and from it, with the program's rules, saturation derives
;;; the pair. `(p a)` alone is root's, and root is alive: no refutation.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what holds since ─────────────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.21, R as above, exit 0; and N's core `(p a)` alone, refuted
;;; by `(given)` and `neg`, in `--dump-answer`'s `nogoods.json`,
;;; `--dump-lattice`'s `nodes.json`, `--events`' `enter` line, `--trace` and
;;; `ein oracle --dump` (`ein solve FILE --dump-answer DIR` at `cdcc3994`,
;;; release and debug). The core search explained each contradiction by its
;;; witness alone, and a pair's witness is its positive side
;;; (`contradiction::Contradiction::witness`). Since S1e2.2.21's fix it
;;; explains a contradiction's sides together, and N's core is `(p a) (q a)`,
;;; in either build.

(relation p T :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")

(p a)

(hrule g () :match (p ?x) :assert (q ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}: guess that q holds of {?x} too")

(rule neg () :match (q ?x) :assert (not (p ?x)) :why "q holds of {?x}, so p does not hold of {?x}")
